<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Nations in Canada</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">First Nations is the name used for the Indigenous peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> other than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a>, made up of hundreds of distinct nations such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lekwungen">Lekwungen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nlakapamux">Nlaka'pamux</a> and Tsilhqot'in of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a>. Colonial governments made land agreements with them, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douglas-treaties">Douglas Treaties</a> on Vancouver Island and the numbered treaties of the prairies, while settlement, epidemics and residential schools dispossessed them. The term came into wide use in the 1980s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-nations-in-canada.jpg" alt="First Nations in Canada" width="320" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dragon401k · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Distribution_of_First_Nations_Canadians_(2021).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
